course: dictionary
lesson: 4106
title: Giveaway: a free prize or a clue?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. «Fake giveaways promise expensive prizes to strangers.» Here a giveaway is a free prize, because the words prizes and promise tell you it is something offered for free.
explain: Another meaning is a small sign that shows the truth or tells a secret. «Her red face was a giveaway.» Her red face showed people what she was feeling, even though she did not say it. A dead giveaway is a very clear clue.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «A real giveaway never asks for your password.» If you swap in free prize, the sentence still makes sense: a real free prize never asks for your password. So the first meaning fits here.
fact: A giveaway is something given for free, such as a prize in a competition.
fact: A dead giveaway is a clear sign that shows the truth or tells a secret.
fact: In informal speech, giveaway can be an adjective: giveaway prices are very low.
fact: The verb «give away» means to give something for free, or to tell a secret by mistake.
fact: «Giveaways asking for card details are scams.» A real giveaway never asks you for your password, so be careful online.
exercise: «Fake giveaways promise expensive prizes to strangers.» What is a giveaway in this sentence? | *A free prize offer | A secret told by mistake | A sign that shows the truth | A sale with high prices
exercise: «Her red face was a giveaway.» What does giveaway mean here? | A very low price | A prize for winning a race | A free gift | *A sign that shows the truth
exercise: Mia says, «The giveaway prices in the sale are great.» What does giveaway mean here? | *Very low, like a bargain | Given away for free | Lost in the post | A hidden clue
test: «A real giveaway never asks for your password.» What should you do if a giveaway asks for your password? | Ask a friend what to do after you have shared it | Give your password and your card details | Type it in quickly to enter | *Refuse, because a real giveaway never asks for it
test: Which phrase means a very clear clue that shows who did it? | *a dead giveaway | give away a gift | enter a giveaway | a free giveaway
exam: Which sentence uses giveaway to mean a free prize? | "Her red face was a giveaway." | "The dog's muddy paws were a giveaway." | *"Enter the giveaway and you might win a bike."
